.TH EUCLID-WRK 8 "October 2026" "euclid 1.0.53" "Euclid System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-wrk \- Euclid worker node: runs the applications euclid places on this host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-wrk
[\fB\-\-config\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-node\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-label\fR|\fB\-l\fR \fIKEY\fR[=\fIVALUE\fR]]...
[\fB\-\-address\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIIP\fR]
[\fB\-\-data\-dir\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-tick\fR|\fB\-t\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-\-ca\-cert\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-credentials\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B euclid-wrk
turns a host into a node that a Euclid installation can place
applications on. It registers with the manager, renews its registration
every tick, and on each renewal is told which application instances this
node should be running. It starts the ones it is not running yet, stops
the ones it should no longer run, and reports each one back.
.PP
It decides nothing itself. How many instances an application runs, and
which node the next one goes on, are the manager's decisions; a worker
reconciles toward what it is told. Nor is a worker host a Euclid host: it
runs no modules, holds no database credentials and listens on nothing.
It reaches the manager's gateway over signed HTTPS as an ordinary Euclid
client, logged in as this node's own principal.
.PP
An application is only placed on nodes when its definition asks for it,
by naming the labels a node has to carry \- see
.I nodeLabels
in
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1).
Every other application keeps running on the manager.
.SS The lease
Every instance a node runs is held on a lease, renewed by each successful
tick. A worker that cannot reach the manager keeps its instances running
until the lease runs out by its own clock \- 45 seconds by default \-
and then stops all of them. The manager waits for the same deadline plus
a margin before placing that work elsewhere, so for a short while nobody
runs it, and never are two copies running. The worker stays up and keeps
trying, and when contact comes back the next renewal says what it should
be running now.
.PP
Asked to stop
.RB ( SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGINT ,
or a service stop), a worker stops its instances and reports them before
it exits, so the manager does not have to wait out a lease to know they
are gone.
.SS What the node reports
On registration the worker reports its name, its labels, the IP address
it reaches the manager from (or \fB\-\-address\fR), the number of CPUs,
its euclid version, and the operating system and CPU architecture it was
built for. The last two are matched by placement as the labels
.I os
\fR(\fIlinux\fR, \fIwindows\fR, \fImacos\fR) and
.I arch
\fR(\fIx86_64\fR, \fIaarch64\fR, \fIarm\fR, ...). They are taken from the
binary rather than the configuration, so a node cannot be mislabelled
into running a native build for another platform, and configured labels
of those names are overridden.
.PP
With every renewal it also reports its one-minute load average, which
placement uses to choose between otherwise equal nodes.
.SS Runtimes
An application starts on a node exactly as it would on the manager:
.TP
.IR JAVA ", " JAVA21 ", " JAVA25
.BI "java -jar " artifact\fR,
with the
.B java
configured for that version.
.TP
.I PYTHON
.BI "python3 " artifact\fR.
.TP
.I NODEJS
.BI "node " artifact\fR.
.TP
.I BINARY
The artifact itself, made executable.
.PP
An application that names its own
.I command
is started with that instead, the artifact as its first argument. The
application's arguments follow in every case. The interpreters have to be
installed on the node; where each one is, is configured per node \- see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.PP
The artifact is fetched from the application's bucket into the data
directory, and fetched again whenever the application's definition
changes, so a redeploy starts the new build.
.PP
Each instance runs in its own process group, in its application's
directory, as the worker's own user, with the
.I EUCLID_*
environment and a credentials file the manager issues for it. The
credentials are renewed halfway through their life on every tick, so a
long-running instance never holds an expired token.
.PP
A worker on Windows installs and registers as a service but does not yet
start applications; an assignment it receives fails rather than being
half-run.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option can be given on the command line. Those marked
.I (config)
can also be set in the configuration file, which is where the shipped
service expects them: the unit file and the launchd job are overwritten
by an upgrade, the configuration file is not. The command line wins.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Configuration file to read. The packaged service uses
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid-wrk.json .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
The manager's gateway, e.g. \fIhttps://euclid.example:5566\fR. Required.
.I (config:
.IR euclid.worker.endpoint )
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-node " " \fINAME\fR
What this node calls itself. Defaults to the host name. The first
principal to register a name owns it: a worker logged in as anybody else
is refused that name until an administrator deletes the registration
with
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-node (1).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " " \fIKEY\fR[=\fIVALUE\fR]
A label for placement to match an application's
.I nodeLabels
against \- a GPU, a licence dongle, a network the node can reach.
Repeatable. A bare \fIKEY\fR means \fIKEY\fR=\fItrue\fR.
.PP
.B \-\-node
and
.B \-\-label
are read from the command line only. For the packaged service, add them
to the unit's
.B ExecStart
with
.BR "systemctl edit euclid-wrk" ,
which writes an override an upgrade leaves alone.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " " \fIIP\fR
The IP address to report for this node. By default the worker asks the
routing table which local address it reaches the manager from, again on
every registration; set this when that is not the address the manager's
side sees, e.g. behind NAT.
.I (config:
.IR euclid.worker.address )
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-data\-dir " " \fIPATH\fR
Where fetched artifacts, instance credentials and application output go.
Defaults to
.IR /var/lib/euclid-wrk ;
the macOS package sets
.IR /usr/local/var/euclid-wrk .
Nothing in it is durable and it can be deleted while the worker is
stopped.
.I (config:
.IR euclid.worker.data-dir )
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tick " " \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds between renewals. Defaults to 10, against a 45 second lease.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
A PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system store, for a
gateway with a self-signed certificate.
.I (config:
.IR euclid.worker.ca-cert )
.TP
.BR \-\-credentials " " \fIPATH\fR
Credentials file to read instead of the invoking user's own
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
.I (config:
.IR euclid.worker.credentials )
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options and exit.
.PP
On Windows only:
.TP
.B \-\-install
Register this executable as the Windows service \fIeuclid-wrk\fR
(automatic start, not started) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Stop and remove the service and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Run as an ordinary console process instead of under the Service Control
Manager.
.SH CONFIGURATION
A JSON file, by default
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid-wrk.json :
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "euclid": {
    "worker": {
      "endpoint": "https://euclid.example:5566",
      "ca-cert": "",
      "address": "",
      "runtimes": {
        "java": "java",
        "java21": "/usr/lib/jvm/java-21-openjdk/bin/java",
        "java25": "/usr/lib/jvm/java-25-openjdk/bin/java",
        "python": "python3",
        "nodejs": "node"
      }
    },
    "logging": {
      "level": "info",
      "dir": "/var/lib/euclid-wrk/log",
      "prefix": "euclid-wrk"
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IR euclid.worker.runtimes.java ", " .java21 ", " .java25 ", " .python ", " .nodejs
The interpreter each runtime is started with on this node. Unset, they
are \fIjava\fR, \fIpython3\fR and \fInode\fR on the
.BR PATH ,
and \fIjava21\fR and \fIjava25\fR \- names no distribution ships, so an
application asking for a Java version the node was never told about
fails to start with that name in the log, rather than running under
another JDK.
.TP
.I euclid.worker.java
The older name for
.IR euclid.worker.runtimes.java ,
still read when the newer one is not set.
.PP
Never point a worker at the manager's
.IR euclid.json .
That file holds
.IR euclid.modules.eam.jwt-secret ,
and anything holding it can mint a token for any principal, administrator
included, offline and unaudited. A worker refuses to start if its
configuration contains that key at all.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
A worker is logged in like any other client, as a principal of its own:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo -H -u euclid-wrk euclid-cli --endpoint https://euclid.example:5566 \e
    eam login --user <this node's principal> --password <secret>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-H
puts the credentials in the
.I euclid-wrk
account's home,
.IR /var/lib/euclid-wrk ,
which is where the service reads them. The principal needs the
.I eap:register-node
and
.I eap:renew-node
actions, and the others a node calls on its own behalf:
.IR eap:issue-instance-credentials ,
.I eap:report-node-instance
and the ESM reads that fetch an application's artifact.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped on request.
.TP
.B 1
The configuration could not be loaded or contains the signing secret,
there are no credentials, no endpoint was given, the CA certificate does
not exist, or the first registration failed. The reason is printed to
standard error.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/bin/euclid-wrk
The executable, linked from
.IR /usr/local/bin/euclid-wrk .
.TP
.I /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid-wrk.json
The configuration file the packaged service reads.
.TP
.I /var/lib/euclid-wrk
The data directory and the
.I euclid-wrk
account's home: artifacts under
.IR applications/ ,
logs under
.IR log/ ,
and the worker's own credentials under
.IR .euclid/ .
.TP
.I /usr/local/var/euclid-wrk
On macOS, the
.I _euclid-wrk
account's home: its log, its credentials and launchd's capture of the
worker's console output.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/euclid-wrk.service
The systemd unit. Installed enabled but not started, since a worker can
do nothing until it has an endpoint and a login.
.TP
.I /Library/LaunchDaemons/de.jensvogt.euclid-wrk.plist
The launchd job on macOS, running as
.IR _euclid-wrk .
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up a node after installing the package:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudoedit /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid-wrk.json      # euclid.worker.endpoint
sudo -H -u euclid-wrk euclid-cli --endpoint https://euclid.example:5566 \e
    eam login --user raspi-01 --password <secret>
sudo systemctl start euclid-wrk
euclid-cli eap get-node --node raspi-01             # from any admin session
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run one by hand, with a label, against a gateway with a self-signed
certificate:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-wrk --endpoint https://euclid.example:5566 \e
    --ca-cert ./euclid_cert.crt --label gpu --data-dir /tmp/wrk
.fi
.RE
